course: language
lesson: 30
title: Texting, abbreviations and emoji
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Texting is a new kind of writing that behaves a lot like talking: short, fast, and full of codes like "brb", "lol" and little pictures called emoji.
explain: Ava sends her gran a 💀 because a video is hilarious ("I'm dead, that's so funny"), and Gran rings in a panic thinking someone has died; the same picture meant two things to two generations.
explain: Short forms are much older than phones: "O.M.G." turns up in a letter from 1917, and the sideways smiley :-) was suggested in 1982, before most people had ever seen a mobile.
explain: Because texts have no voice or face, capitals, emoji and abbreviations do the job of tone, and different people read them differently, so think about who you're texting.
fact: LOL means "laughing out loud"; some older people once thought it meant "lots of love", and today it is often used just to sound friendly.
fact: "O.M.G. (Oh! My God!)" was written by a British admiral, Lord Fisher, in a letter to Winston Churchill in 1917, one of the earliest known written uses.
fact: Many young people use 💀 to mean "I'm dying of laughter"; older people may read it as being about death.
fact: "Emoji" is Japanese: "e" (picture) plus "moji" (character); early emoji appeared on Japanese phones in the late 1990s, including a famous set by Shigetaka Kurita in 1999.
fact: Emoticons are faces made from keyboard characters, like :-) and :-( ; the computer scientist Scott Fahlman suggested them in 1982.
fact: Surveys reported in the news say some young people read the 👍 emoji as cold or annoyed, while many older people use it just to mean OK.
fact: In 2015 the Oxford Dictionaries "Word of the Year" was an emoji, 😂, the face with tears of joy, because it was the most used that year.
fact: Writing in all capitals usually feels like shouting in a text, but some older people use capitals because they are easier to read.
fact: "brb" means be right back, "gtg" means got to go, and "ttyl" means talk to you later.
fact: Some studies, including research in Britain, found that children who used text abbreviations were not worse at spelling, and some were better; scientists still argue about it.
exercise: Mum texts "LOL, Grandma's coming!" What does LOL mean? | Laughing out loud | Lots of love | Look out, Leo | Lunch on Saturday
exercise: What did some older people once think LOL meant? | Lots of love | Lots of laughs | Lost or late | Lights off later
exercise: When was "O.M.G." written in a letter to Winston Churchill? | 1917 | 1997 | 2007 | 1817
exercise: What did the 1917 letter show? | Short forms are much older than phones | Churchill invented texting on an early telephone | Admirals had mobile phones | OMG was a secret army code used by the navy
exercise: Ava sends her gran 💀 about a funny video. What does Ava mean? | That's so funny, I'm dead laughing | Someone in the family has died | I'm scared of the video | It's Halloween
exercise: Where does the word "emoji" come from? | Japanese for picture + character | English "emotion" | French "émoi" | Latin "emo"
exercise: What is :-) called? | An emoticon, a face made from keyboard characters | An emoji picture drawn by a Japanese phone company | A hashtag | An abbreviation
exercise: Who suggested :-) and :-( in 1982? | Scott Fahlman | Shigetaka Kurita | Lord Fisher | William Caxton
exercise: Leo sends 👍 to his older cousin, who thinks Leo is annoyed. What's going on? | Some read 👍 as cold, others just as OK | 👍 always means angry, everywhere and for everyone | 👍 means "go away" everywhere | The cousin's phone showed a different picture
exercise: What was the Oxford Dictionaries "Word of the Year" in 2015? | 😂, the face with tears of joy | Selfie | LOL | 💀
exercise: Gran texts "WHERE ARE YOU." Why might Mia think Gran is shouting? | All capitals usually feel like shouting in texts | Gran used a full stop at the end, which always means anger | Gran used too many words | Gran sent it at night
exercise: Why might Gran really use capitals? | They're easier for her to read | She is always furious | Her phone has no small letters | She wants to win a prize
exercise: What does "gtg" mean? | Got to go | Good to go | Great, thanks, Gran | Get the game
exercise: What does "ttyl" mean? | Talk to you later | Thanks, take your lunch | Too tired, yawn lots | Text the young lad
exercise: Grace asks if texting makes kids bad at spelling. What did some studies find? | Texting kids were not worse, and some were better | Texting always ruins spelling, in every child who does it | Texting kids can't spell at all | No studies have ever been done
test: Your mate goes "OMG is a phone word, invented like 2005, innit." What's true? | It was written in a letter in 1917 | He's right, it's from 2005 | It was invented by a game | It's from an old Latin book
test: Your gran texts "So sorry about Goldie LOL" after your goldfish died. What has she probably mixed up? | She thinks LOL means "lots of love" | She thinks your goldfish was a joke and is laughing at you | She is laughing at you | She can't read texts
test: What would happen if you sent 💀 to someone who only knows the old meaning? | They might think something terrible happened | They'd always laugh, because 💀 is funny to everybody | Their phone would crash | Nothing, everyone reads it the same
test: Your Canadian cousin texts "brb, grabbing a double-double eh." ("Double-double" is a coffee with two creams and two sugars.) What does "brb" tell you? | He'll be right back | He's going to bed | He's being rude | He's got to go for good
test: A cartoon says "Emoji means EMOTION icon!" What's the real story? | It's Japanese: picture + character | It's right, from English | It's from Spanish | It's from Ukrainian
test: Your teenage sister says "a thumbs up from you is giving 'whatever', it's lowkey rude." ("It's giving" means it seems like; "lowkey" means a bit.) What's the wise move? | Remember some people read 👍 as cold, and add a word if needed | Never text her again | Tell her emoji have one fixed meaning, written down in a big official rule book | Only use capitals from now on
test: Dad says "😂 can't be Word of the Year, it's not even a word!" What's true? | It was chosen in 2015 because it was used so much | Dad's right, it never happened | It was Word of the Year in 1917 | It was chosen as Picture of the Year, not Word of the Year
test: Your mum reads your mate's text "gtg ttyl" and asks if it's code for something bad. What should you say? | It means "got to go, talk to you later" | It is secret spy code | It's a spelling mistake | It means "go to the garden"
test: Your mate says "texting wrecks spelling, everyone knows it." What's fairest? | Some studies found texting kids were no worse, but scientists still argue | He's right, it's fully proven by every study ever done on children and phones | Texting makes everyone a perfect speller | Spelling doesn't matter at all
test: Isaac's dad turns his head sideways to read ":-(". What does it show? | A sad face made from keyboard characters | An angry robot | A smiling sun | A secret password
test: Your grandad in Yorkshire texts "SITTING IN T'CAR, WHERE ARE YOU LOT." ("T'" means the.) Should you assume he's furious? | Not necessarily; many older people type in capitals to read easily | Yes, capitals always mean anger, whoever types them and whatever their age | Yes, Yorkshire people are always cross | No, capitals mean he is happy
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the origin of the word "emoji"? | It derives from the Japanese for "picture" and "character" | It derives from the English word "emotion" | It derives from the French "émoi" | It derives from the Latin "emovere"
exam: Identify the principal significance of a letter written by Lord Fisher in 1917 containing "O.M.G.". | It demonstrates that such abbreviations predate mobile telephones | It constitutes the earliest evidence for the invention of the mobile telephone | It proves that Churchill devised text messaging | It establishes that abbreviations were then prohibited
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the effect of text abbreviations upon children's spelling. | Certain studies found no detriment, though the matter remains disputed | It is conclusively proven that spelling is destroyed | It is conclusively proven that spelling is perfected | No investigation of the question has ever been undertaken
exam: Ascertain the meaning of the abbreviation "brb". | Be right back | Bring round bread | Bye, really bored | Best regards, brother
